In a previous conversation, James sought to cite a paper pertinent to a discussion on adaptation in fiscal planning for climate change, prompted by a reviewer’s comment. I suggested two potential placements for the citation of the World Bank paper. First, it could be integrated where existing modeling limitations are mentioned, providing context to the analytical challenges Ministries of Finance face. Second, it could be included in the discussion about the necessity for diverse methodologies beyond traditional models, where the World Bank's integrated approach is advocated. This incorporation would address the reviewer's suggestion for more concrete tools and frameworks, enhancing the argument for integrating adaptation into macro-fiscal analyses.
